            <ArticleTitle>Diflunisal versus placebo for treatment of pain in general practice.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>85-92</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The efficacy and safety of diflunisal versus placebo in the treatment of pain were compared in a multicenter, double-blind study of 1,037 patients treated for seven days by general practitioners. A statistically significant (P less than 0.001) difference in favor of diflunisal was seen using four evaLuation criteria. The incidence of side effects was generally low with both treatments; nevertheless, the number of patients suffering at least one side effect was greater after diflunisal than after placebo (P less than 0.05). A comparison of the profile of responders to diflunisal and to placebo showed no particular pattern.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
